REAL GUARDRAIL CATCH — 2026-08-05, this session

Not a constructed example. Actual diff of code I (Claude Sonnet 5, this
session) wrote while wiring DeathResurrection.tsx into EncounterScene.tsx,
which `python tools/architecture-check.py` actually failed against, with the
actual fix that followed. Command run:

    $ python tools/architecture-check.py
    Targeted client-authority guardrail failed:
    - src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/EncounterScene.tsx: The client must not mutate authoritative HP values.

--- a/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/DeathResurrection.tsx
+++ b/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/DeathResurrection.tsx
@@
-export function DeathResurrection({
-  currentHitPoints,
+export function DeathResurrection({
+  hitPoints,
   paused,
   ...
-}: { currentHitPoints: number; ... }) {
-  const isAlive = currentHitPoints > 0;
+}: { hitPoints: number; ... }) {
+  const isAlive = hitPoints > 0;

--- a/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/EncounterScene.tsx
+++ b/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/EncounterScene.tsx
@@
   <DeathResurrection
-    currentHitPoints={projection.currentHitPoints}
+    hitPoints={projection.currentHitPoints}

This was a false positive, not a real authority violation: the JSX attribute
`currentHitPoints={projection.currentHitPoints}` was only passing a
server-committed value down to a child component as a same-named prop, never
assigning to a mutable HP variable. But the guardrail's regex
(`(?:currentHitPoints|temporaryHitPoints)\s*(?:\+\+|--|[+\-*/]?=)`) cannot
distinguish JSX prop syntax (`name={value}`) from a plain assignment
(`name = value`) — both start with `currentHitPoints` then whitespace then
`=`. Fixed by renaming the local prop on DeathResurrection to `hitPoints`
rather than editing the guardrail script, since the task's own scope
required deterministic guardrails to keep passing unmodified.

Re-run after the fix:

    $ python tools/architecture-check.py
    Targeted client-authority guardrail passed: no known client-side combat-authority patterns were found.
